  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "            The notes on the basic sentences introduce a number of polite expressions and forms. \n\n\"Con permiso\" is used to politely ask permission to pass by or excuse oneself. \n\"Dispénseme\" is used to apologise for a minor breach of etiquette, such as accidentally bumping into someone. \n\"Muchas\" is the feminine plural form of \"mucho\" and is used with feminine plural nouns, for example, \"Muchas gracias\" (many thanks). \nThe expression \"No hay de qué\" means \"you're welcome\" or \"don't mention it\". \nWhen a man meets a woman, he says \"Encantado de conocerla\" to mean \"Pleased to meet you\"; a woman meeting a man says \"Mucho gusto de conocerlo\". \nThese nuances are important when greeting and showing courtesy in Spanish.\n"
    }
  ],
